    <value>It needs at least {0} GB of memory and {1} GB of disk space to be installed.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Reason_Beginner" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Made for beginners: everything is done with the mouse</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Reason_Lightweight" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Lightweight: stays fast even on an old PC</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Reason_Gaming" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Good for games: recent drivers, Steam is easy to install</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Reason_Development" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Liked by developers: recent tools that are easy to install</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Reason_LikeWindows" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Looks like Windows: you will not feel lost</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Reason_Modern" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A modern and clean desktop</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Reason_Stable" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Stable: updates are tested for a long time before they arrive</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Reason_Latest" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Always the latest versions of the software</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Reason_SecureBoot" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Starts without changing anything in the BIOS</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Simple_ubuntu-desktop" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The most used version of Linux in the world. It is backed by a company, help is everywhere, and it gets security updates for 5 years.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Simple_lubuntu" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A lighter Ubuntu: a very simple desktop that needs few resources, ideal to bring an old computer back to life.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Simple_debian-live" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>One of the oldest distributions, known for being rock solid: it changes little and almost never breaks. A little less guided for beginners.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Simple_linuxmint-cinnamon" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The choice most often recommended to people leaving Windows: the menu, the taskbar and the windows work like on Windows, and everything is ready to use.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Simple_linuxmint-xfce" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A lighter Linux Mint: the same simplicity, close to Windows, but faster on old or modest computers.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Simple_fedora-workstation" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Modern and polished, with the very latest versions of the software twice a year. Much liked by developers and enthusiasts.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Simple_manjaro-kde" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Always up to date, with a very customizable KDE desktop that recalls Windows. For those who like the latest features and some tinkering.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Simple_opensuse-leap" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A serious and stable distribution with a complete configuration tool. Rather for people who already have some experience.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Simple_opensuse-tumbleweed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Updated continuously, yet automatically tested before every update. For confident users who want the latest versions.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Simple_popos" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Made by a computer maker, it is simple, good looking and very good for games and work. It needs Secure Boot to be turned off in the BIOS.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Simple_zorin-core" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Designed for people coming from Windows: its desktop looks a lot like it, and it is very easy to get started with.</value>
  </data>
